#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069617} :activity/id #uuid "10771a71-8e46-50cc-b75f-ab2b84a9afeb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048187 :topic/id #uuid "86a29d40-d28e-53d5-b56d-cdcc72f7fe80" :topic/math-academy-id 430} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069617 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070019 :tutorial/content "When an equation contains an $x^2$ term and a constant, it can be solved using the **square root method**. The square root method makes use of the fact that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = \\vert x \\vert \\,.\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, we can solve the equation $2x^2-18=0$ using the following three steps:\n\n1\\. Isolate the $x^2$ on one side of the equation:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-18 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Take the square root of both sides of the equation\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ |x| & =3\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}x & =3\\,\\text{or}\\,x=-3\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\nSo, there are two solutions, $x=3$ and $x=-3.$ We can write this more concisely as $x= \\pm 3,$ where the \"$\\pm$\" symbol stands for \"plus or minus.\"" :tutorial/id #uuid "7ef92091-e041-5715-ac4e-75fa146c4cea" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8692 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6a92a024-3077-5b06-9926-573d76e002f6" :step/math-academy-id 165690 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069618}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068725 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068475 :question/id #uuid "fed35802-1128-5cb4-a91b-e98227dcfba8" :question/math-academy-id "e-13966"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f544d0a4-24ce-5078-ad9d-93e733c091e6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049843}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069222} {:db/